pe":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is 0, ⊕ is ","type":"text"},{"code":"min","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ⊗ is ","type":"text"},{"code":"+","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". ","type":"text"},{"code":"<","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" compares the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"values, so shorter is better.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"On integers ⊗ is a closed sum. ","type":"text"},{"code":".max","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (+∞, the zero) absorbs, then ","type":"text"},{"code":".min","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (−∞), and any other","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"sum traps on overflow. A finite sum that lands exactly on ","type":"text"},{"code":".max","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the zero, so an edge of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"that weight carries no path.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"On floating point the carrier is ℝ ∪ {+∞}, and ⊗ is ","type":"text"},{"code":"+","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", where ","type":"text"},{"code":".infinity","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" absorbs every finite","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"value. −∞ is outside the carrier, and ","type":"text"},{"code":"-.infinity ⊗ .zero","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is NaN, not ","type":"text"},{"code":".zero","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". No algorithm","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"here computes that product, because each skips a ","type":"text"},{"code":".zero","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" distance and treats a NaN candidate as","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"no improvement. No operation checks for NaN. The ShortestPaths entry points trap on a NaN","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"weight.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":null,"type":"codeListing","code":["graph.dijkstraShortestPaths(from: s) { TropicalSemiring(length[$0]) }"]}],"kind":"content"}],"references":{"doc://Semi